[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1, dswa2, dswb1, dswb2
diagnosis_text:
The affected component is the DHCP relay function on all distribution switches. The isc-dhcp-relay daemon is not running and has no configuration, so client DHCPDISCOVER broadcasts on the access VLAN SVIs are never forwarded to the DHCP server.

Mechanism: Existing clients keep working because they already have valid leases and default gateways, but newly booted hosts depend on the relay to reach the DHCP server in VLAN 30 (10.5.0.0/24). With the relay down/unconfigured, their broadcasts remain local and time out.

Localization: On dswa1, dswa2, dswb1, and dswb2:
- systemctl status shows isc-dhcp-relay “inactive (dead)” on all four.
- /etc/default/isc-dhcp-relay shows SERVERS="" and INTERFACES="" on all four devices (empty), meaning no helper-targets and no listening interfaces are defined.
The SVIs that should be relayed are present and up (e.g., dswa1 br0.10=10.1.0.2/24, br0.20=10.2.0.2/24, br0.40=10.6.0.2/24; dswb1 br0.10=10.3.0.2/24, br0.20=10.4.0.2/24, br0.30=10.5.0.2/24). The DHCP server host srv1 is reachable at 10.5.0.4/24.

Root cause: A recent distribution-switch change wiped the isc-dhcp-relay configuration and left the service disabled on all distribution switches.

Corrective fix: On dswa1, dswa2, dswb1, and dswb2, populate /etc/default/isc-dhcp-relay with SERVERS="10.5.0.4" and INTERFACES listing the client VLAN SVIs (e.g., “br0.10 br0.20 br0.30 br0.40” as appropriate), then enable and start the service (systemctl enable --now isc-dhcp-relay). Verify UDP 67/68 permitted and test a fresh client boot to confirm lease acquisition. === END ===